EXT. WELL-DIGGING CAMP - DESERT - DAY

A dry plain under a white sky. A scaffold of timbers stands over a deep shaft. A windlass creaks. A water barrel stands empty in the shade of a tilting wagon.

EZRA LOCKRIDGE, 50s, sunburnt, sleeves rolled, hauls a bucket of rock and clay up from the shaft hand over hand. MERCY TAVARES, 30s, in a broad straw hat, stands at the lip of the pit and holds the rope steady. She is thin, tireless, and has not spoken for a long time.

The bucket clears the rim. Ezra tips it. A thin trickle of mud slides out. He kneels, takes a pinch, and rubs it between his fingers.

EZRA
Wet.

MERCY
Wet isn't water.

EZRA
It's near.

MERCY
You said that yesterday.

He wipes his hands on his trousers. He rises, stiff. The wind carries a dull sound from the east, the clop of a single horse.

They turn. A rider approaches along the dry riverbed, slowly, a long shadow thrown ahead of him. He is tall and spare, with a black coat and a silver star on his lapel. ANSEL WHITCOMBE, 40s, a land agent, reins in at the edge of camp and dismounts.

ANSEL
Morning.

EZRA
It's near noon.

ANSEL
So it is. I'm told you've been digging on the Culver claim.

EZRA
I'm told it's nobody's claim.

ANSEL
It's the bank's, as of Tuesday.

Mercy lets the rope go slack. Ezra does not move. The windlass creaks as the bucket swings.

MERCY
We have a filed claim.

ANSEL
You have a filed claim in the wrong county.

EZRA
That so.

ANSEL
I'm afraid it is.

He takes a folded paper from his coat and holds it out. Ezra does not take it. Ansel sets it on the windlass crank and steps back.

ANSEL (CONT'D)
You have until sundown.

MERCY
There's water down there.

ANSEL
There's mud.

MERCY
It's water, mister. Give us an hour.

Ansel looks at the shaft, then at the horizon. He sighs and removes his hat. Sweat runs from his hair.

ANSEL
One hour. Then I'm obliged to read it aloud.

He walks to the shade of the wagon and sits on its tongue. Ezra waits. Then he climbs back down the ladder into the shaft. Mercy takes up the rope again, steady as a post, and begins to haul.
